First Author: Barbosa Jr
Title: Machine Learning Algorithms Utilizing Quantitative CT Features May Predict Eventual Onset of Bronchiolitis Obliterans Syndrome After Lung Transplantation
Source number: 5  ￼

Very short summary: This retrospective study evaluated whether quantitative CT (qCT) features and machine learning could predict later bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome (BOS) after lung transplantation. Five key findings were: (1) 71 lung transplant recipients were analyzed overall, including 41 BOS and 30 non-BOS patients; (2) 23 baseline qCT parameters significantly distinguished future BOS developers from non-BOS developers, while baseline pulmonary function test parameters did not; (3) support vector machine models using baseline qCT features predicted future BOS with up to 85% accuracy using 3 features; (4) in BOS patients, FEV1 decline was associated with increased lung volume at FRC and increased central airway volume at FRC; and (5) in non-BOS patients, FEV1 decline was associated instead with decreased central airway volume at TLC. Tables and figures were used directly for this extraction, especially Tables 1–5 and Figures 2–8.  ￼  ￼  ￼

