	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: The clinical outcome was eventual BOS after lung transplant, which is the obstructive phenotype of chronic lung allograft dysfunction, so the closest restricted category is CLAD. Table 5 reports the best 3-feature SVM classification accuracy as 85%, using RML lobe volume at TLC, RUL airway resistance at FRC, and central airway surface at FRC. One-feature accuracy reached 76%, and the best two-feature model reached 83%. The paper did not report AUC, sensitivity, specificity, PPV, NPV, F1-score, calibration, or DCA. Figure 7 summarized significant baseline qCT predictors, and Figure 8 showed that baseline FEV1 and FEV1 percent predicted were not significant predictors of future BOS.  ￼

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: This was a small retrospective cohort of 71 patients selected using several imaging and data-availability criteria, and only 41 patients were eligible for the baseline prediction analysis because incomplete datasets were excluded.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors were objective qCT/FRI imaging biomarkers extracted from paired inspiratory-expiratory CT scans with standardized segmentation and computational methods.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: BOS was defined using standard clinical criteria requiring a sustained decline of at least 10% in FEV1 from best baseline postoperative measurement, and BOS status was reviewed clinically by a board-certified pulmonologist.  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The study used a very small baseline prediction sample, selected features using univariate testing before SVM modeling, used default SVM tuning parameters, and reported repeated random split accuracies without an external validation cohort.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: This article directly addresses AI/ML prediction in thoracic transplantation, specifically lung transplant recipients and later BOS/CLAD development using imaging biomarkers.  ￼

